</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Cost of Learning to Learn</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also assess the time and memory overhead incurred during the training of the ZO Fine-Tuner in table <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ C.4 Cost of Learning to Learn ‣ Appendix C Additional Experimental Results ‣ Learning a Zeroth-Order Optimizer for Fine-Tuning LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. In general, L2L takes approximately 2.4× the time of standard first-order fine-tuning. This is expected, as the L2L process inherently includes a full fine-tuning phase using SGD. However, importantly, this cost is incurred only <span id="A3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">once</span>, as a single ZO Fine-Tuner trained for a given model can be reused across diverse downstream tasks, effectively amortizing the training cost.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Time and memory cost of meta-training the ZO Fine-Tuner in our L2L framework.
GPU memory usage and GPU time (in minutes) are reported for different foundation models.
This cost is incurred only once per base model, and the trained fine-tuner can be reused across downstream tasks.</figcaption>
